(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-pleural-disorders-vignettes"]={"title":"Pleural Disorders Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 66-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a symptomatic right pneumothorax on his upright chest radiograph. He is otherwise stable. Which intervention is contraindicated for him?","choices":["Hospital admission","Thoracoscopic surgery","Catheter aspiration","Chest tube insertion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Underlying pulmonary disease contraindicates catheter aspiration, along with traumatic or tension pneumothorax, hemodynamic instability, recurrence, bilateral pneumothoraces and bleeding disorders. His chronic obstructive pulmonary disease makes this a secondary spontaneous pneumothorax, and most of those get a chest tube instead. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 39, 49, 56, 57"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman has a very large left pleural effusion, estimated at more than 1,000 mL on imaging, with marked dyspnea. Which inspection finding is expected?","choices":["Trachea pulled to the left","Symmetric chest expansion","No findings at this size","Trachea shifted to the right"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. With an effusion over 1,000 mL, the trachea shifts away from the involved side, so a left effusion pushes it to the right; inspection also shows asymmetrical thoracic expansion. Small effusions under 300 mL produce no examination findings, but hers is large. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man is brought in after a high-speed motor vehicle crash. He is in severe respiratory distress, with blood pressure 78/50 mm Hg and heart rate 136 beats per minute. His trachea is deviated to the left, his neck veins are distended, his right chest is hyperresonant and breath sounds are absent on the right. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Tension pneumothorax","Hemothorax","Pneumomediastinum","Open pneumothorax"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Air enters the pleural space but cannot escape, a one-way valve, causing severe ventilatory compromise and a mediastinal shift to the contralateral side. Decreased venous return and cardiac output follow, with hypotension, tracheal deviation, neck vein distention and absent breath sounds, his exact picture. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 62, 63, 64, 66, 77, 88"},{"q":"A 25-year-old man presents with his second primary spontaneous pneumothorax on the right; the first was treated 18 months ago. He is stable. Which management best prevents further recurrence?","choices":["Chest tube, then discharge","Thoracoscopic surgery with pleurodesis","Catheter aspiration, then discharge","Observation with 100% oxygen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A recurrent primary spontaneous pneumothorax calls for surgical therapy, video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery with pleurodesis, which is the best preventive procedure. Recurrence is common, approaching 50% within three years after a first episode, and his second episode proves the risk. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 39, 53, 55, 59"},{"q":"A 15-year-old girl has pleurodynia diagnosed during a summer outbreak at her camp, with fever, knife-like pleuritic pain and tender intercostal muscles. Her chest radiograph is normal. Which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":["Therapeutic thoracentesis","Anti-inflammatory drug and heat","Chest tube drainage","Oxygen by non-rebreather"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Treatment of pleurodynia is supportive: nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and application of heat to the affected muscles. The course is typically benign, though symptoms may recur a few days later, and hand hygiene limits fecal-oral spread. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 53, 96, 118, 119"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with cystic fibrosis has had two pneumothoraces in a year. She is stable, and surgery is being planned, so her team wants the most complete picture of the size and location of the pleural air and of any blebs or bullae in the underlying lung. Which imaging test is best?","choices":["Upright chest radiograph","Bedside lung ultrasound","Lateral decubitus film","Chest computed tomography"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Thoracic computed tomography is the best modality for determining the presence, size and location of a pneumothorax, detects blebs and bullae and assesses underlying disease; it is the diagnostic gold standard. It is impractical only when time is critical, and she is stable. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 26, 30, 33, 34, 91"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman in the intensive care unit has a subclavian vein catheter placed for venous access. An hour later she reports new right-sided pleuritic chest pain and dyspnea, and a chest radiograph shows a visceral pleural line on the right. She has no history of lung disease or chest trauma. How is her pneumothorax classified?","choices":["Secondary spontaneous","Iatrogenic","Traumatic","Primary spontaneous"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Iatrogenic pneumothorax is injury to the pleura secondary to a medical intervention, and subclavian vein catheter placement is one of the listed examples, with thoracentesis, bronchoscopy and mechanical ventilation. Her symptoms began an hour after the catheter, with no lung disease or trauma. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man on a ventilator after a car crash cannot sit up, so a supine portable chest radiograph is taken. It shows a deep sulcus sign (a dark, deep costophrenic angle) on the left. What does this finding indicate?","choices":["Pleural air collecting laterally","Pleural fluid at the angle","Air within the mediastinum","Infiltrate in the lower lobe"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The deep sulcus sign is seen on a supine film: a dark and deep costophrenic angle caused by a lateral collection of pleural air. In this patient lying flat, it is how a pneumothorax shows itself. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 29, 75, 77, 89, 90"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with alcohol-related cirrhosis has tense ascites and a new right pleural effusion. He has no orthopnea, leg edema or known heart disease, and no fever or cough. Pleural fluid protein is 1.2 g/dL with serum protein 5.8 g/dL (normal 6.0-8.3 g/dL). Pleural fluid lactate dehydrogenase is 70 units/L with serum lactate dehydrogenase 180 units/L (normal 140-280 units/L). Which is the most likely cause of the effusion?","choices":["Tuberculosis","Cirrhosis","Heart failure","Pneumonia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Protein ratio 1.2 / 5.8 = 0.21, lactate dehydrogenase ratio 70 / 180 = 0.39, and fluid lactate dehydrogenase 70 is under two-thirds of 280, about 187: all of Light's criteria are absent, a transudate. Cirrhosis with ascites sends peritoneal fluid across the diaphragm and lowers oncotic pressure. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 81, 82, 101, 103, 110, 111"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman with a hemothorax after a stab wound has a chest tube placed. Over the next 2 hours it drains blood at about 300 mL per hour, and her blood pressure is falling. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Chemical pleurodesis","Needle decompression","Thoracotomy","Continued tube drainage"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The chest tube evacuates and quantifies the bleeding, and blood loss over 200 mL per hour calls for thoracotomy, an open approach to the intrathoracic space. Persistent hemorrhage or shock is the indication for surgery, and she has both. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 45, 66, 69, 74"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman has sudden left-sided pleuritic chest pain and dyspnea at rest. Her upright chest radiograph is reviewed for a pneumothorax. Which radiographic finding establishes the diagnosis?","choices":["Blunted costophrenic angle","Straight air-fluid line","Markings beyond the pleural edge","Visceral pleural line"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The diagnosis is established by demonstrating a visceral pleural line, the interface of lung and pleural air. It is a thin, sharp line convex outward toward the chest wall, with no bronchovascular markings visible beyond it. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 26, 71, 90"},{"q":"A 25-year-old man has a stab wound to the right chest wall. Air can be heard moving through the wound, which draws air into the pleural space with each inhalation, and breath sounds are diminished on the right. How is his pneumothorax classified?","choices":["Primary spontaneous","Iatrogenic","Open pneumothorax","Closed pneumothorax"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An open pneumothorax has a direct wound in the chest wall that allows air to enter the pleural space during inhalation, and it can lead to a sucking chest wound. A penetrating stab wound is a traumatic cause. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 17, 61, 62"},{"q":"A 23-year-old man has a first primary spontaneous pneumothorax with an apex-to-cupola distance of 2 cm on his upright chest radiograph. He has mild pleuritic pain, no significant dyspnea and stable vital signs. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Observation with 100% oxygen","Chest tube insertion","Catheter aspiration","Thoracoscopic pleurodesis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A small primary spontaneous pneumothorax, under 3 cm at the apex, with minimal symptoms is observed with adequate analgesia and oxygen at 100% by non-rebreather mask, which accelerates resorption of air. A repeat film confirms it has not expanded, and he is discharged if it is stable and he is asymptomatic. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 53, 54, 55"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man has 5 days of fever, cough with purulent sputum and right pleuritic chest pain, and a right pleural effusion is tapped. Pleural fluid protein is 4.4 g/dL with serum protein 7.2 g/dL (normal 6.0-8.3 g/dL). Pleural fluid lactate dehydrogenase is 240 units/L with serum lactate dehydrogenase 210 units/L (normal 140-280 units/L). Which is the most likely cause of the effusion?","choices":["Nephrotic syndrome","Pneumonia","Heart failure","Cirrhosis with ascites"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Protein ratio 4.4 / 7.2 = 0.61, above 0.5; lactate dehydrogenase ratio 240 / 210 = 1.14, above 0.6; fluid lactate dehydrogenase 240 exceeds two-thirds of 280, about 187. The fluid is an exudate, pneumonia is the most common cause of exudative effusion, and fever with purulent sputum and pleuritic pain is its clue set. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 86, 101, 102, 110, 111, 112"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease uses noninvasive positive-pressure ventilation at home every night. He is admitted with a secondary spontaneous pneumothorax, and air is still bubbling through his chest tube, showing an active air leak. Which of his therapies should be held for now?","choices":["Chest tube drainage","Noninvasive ventilation","Supplemental oxygen","Analgesic medication"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Positive-pressure therapies are held during an active air leak. His nightly noninvasive ventilation is positive pressure, and air still bubbling through his tube shows the leak is active, so it is paused until the leak has closed. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 53, 57, 60"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man with severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a second secondary spontaneous pneumothorax, now drained by a chest tube. His team judges that he cannot tolerate general anesthesia, so surgery is not an option. Which procedure best lowers his risk of another recurrence?","choices":["Video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery","Repeated catheter aspiration","Chemical pleurodesis via chest tube","Mechanical pleurodesis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Most secondary spontaneous pneumothoraces are treated with a chest tube and may go on to thoracoscopic surgery or thoracotomy with bleb stapling and pleural abrasion. If surgery is not an option, chemical pleurodesis via the chest tube is used: a sclerosant such as talc makes the pleural layers adhere. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 39, 46, 49, 57"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man on peritoneal dialysis develops a right pleural effusion. He has no heart failure, and his serum albumin is normal at 4.0 g/dL (normal 3.5-5.0 g/dL). He asks how the dialysis fluid in his abdomen could reach his chest. Which explanation is accurate?","choices":["It lowers oncotic pressure","It blocks lymph drainage","It crosses the diaphragm","It raises capillary pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In cirrhosis and peritoneal dialysis, increased peritoneal fluid leaks, or extravasates, across the diaphragm through microperforations into the pleural space. Peritoneal dialysis is listed among the conditions associated with transudates. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 81, 82, 111"},{"q":"A 23-year-old tall, thin man has acute dyspnea and sharp right-sided pleuritic chest pain that began at rest. His respiratory rate is 20 breaths per minute, heart rate 98 beats per minute, blood pressure 122/76 mm Hg and oxygen saturation 96% on room air, and he speaks in whole sentences. Which is the initial test?","choices":["Upright chest radiograph","Chest computed tomography","Arterial blood gas","Supine chest radiograph"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The initial test for pneumothorax is a standard upright chest radiograph. The diagnosis is established by showing the visceral pleural line, a thin, sharp line convex toward the chest wall with no bronchovascular markings beyond it, and his stable vital signs leave time for the film. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 26, 29, 33, 34, 35"},{"q":"A 21-year-old tall, thin man is recovering from his first primary spontaneous pneumothorax. He smokes a pack of cigarettes a day, and his father had the same condition. Before discharge, the clinician counsels him on the risk factor he can change. Which risk factor should the counseling address?","choices":["Cigarette smoking","Family history","Tall, thin build","Strenuous exercise"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Smoking is a significant risk factor for primary spontaneous pneumothorax and the one he can change. Recurrence approaches 50% within three years of a first episode, so education includes the importance of smoking cessation and help to quit if he is willing. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 19, 21, 59, 60"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man with pneumomediastinum after forceful breath-holding while inhaling an illicit drug develops worsening dyspnea, subcutaneous emphysema spreading up his neck and a falling oxygen saturation despite oxygen. His airway is being supported. Which referral is most appropriate?","choices":["Cardiothoracic surgery","Outpatient pulmonology","Genetics referral","Gastroenterology clinic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pneumomediastinum is usually managed conservatively, but if it is severe, cardiothoracic surgery is consulted emergently, after the airway is assessed and maintained. His worsening dyspnea, spreading emphysema and falling saturation despite oxygen mark severe disease. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 60, 75, 76, 79"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man has had sharp, well-localized left chest pain for 3 days after a viral upper respiratory infection. The pain worsens with deep breathing and coughing, and a pleural friction rub (a scratching, creaking sound) disappears when he holds his breath. Myocardial infarction, pneumothorax and pneumonia have been excluded, and his chest radiograph is normal. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Chest tube insertion","Chemical pleurodesis","Non-rebreather oxygen","Anti-inflammatory drug"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pleurisy is treated by treating the underlying condition and giving nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs. His viral infection is an infectious cause, the dangerous causes are excluded, and the anti-inflammatory drug addresses the parietal pleural inflammation behind his pain. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 10, 13, 14, 41, 45, 53"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man with a tension pneumothorax after a gunshot wound has hypotension, tracheal deviation to the right, distended neck veins and absent left breath sounds. His family asks why his blood pressure has fallen. Which mechanism explains his hypotension?","choices":["Blood loss into the chest","Increased cardiac output","Slowed heart rate","Less venous return to the heart"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In tension pneumothorax, air trapped by a one-way valve shifts the mediastinum to the contralateral side. Venous return to the heart decreases, cardiac output falls, and hypotension follows, along with tracheal deviation and neck vein distention. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 63, 64, 69"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman has a new left pleural effusion with no clear clinical cause, and diagnostic thoracentesis is performed. Pleural fluid protein is 2.1 g/dL with serum protein 7.0 g/dL (normal 6.0-8.3 g/dL). Pleural fluid lactate dehydrogenase is 210 units/L with serum lactate dehydrogenase 450 units/L (normal 140-280 units/L). How is the effusion classified by Light's criteria?","choices":["Exudate, by lactate dehydrogenase level","Exudate, by protein ratio","Exudate, by lactate dehydrogenase ratio","Transudate, no criterion met"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Protein ratio 2.1 / 7.0 = 0.30, not above 0.5. Lactate dehydrogenase ratio 210 / 450 = 0.47, not above 0.6. But fluid lactate dehydrogenase 210 exceeds two-thirds of the 280 upper limit of normal serum lactate dehydrogenase, about 187: met, so the fluid is an exudate. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 96, 101, 110, 112"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with hypertension and a 40-pack-year smoking history has sudden sharp left chest pain that worsens with deep breathing. Before the pain is attributed to pleurisy, which condition belongs among those that must be considered immediately?","choices":["Aortic dissection","Costochondritis","Rib fracture","Herpes zoster nerve pain"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pleuritic pain demands immediate consideration of the dangerous causes: myocardial infarction, pericarditis, aortic dissection, pneumonia, pneumothorax and pulmonary embolism. Only after those are weighed are the other causes, such as rib fracture, costochondritis, vertebral fracture or zoster nerve pain, considered. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has a bloody-appearing pleural effusion after a fall. The pleural fluid hematocrit is 30%, and his blood hematocrit is 40% (normal 41-53%). How is the effusion classified?","choices":["Empyema","Hemothorax","Hemorrhagic effusion","Chylothorax"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A pleural fluid hematocrit over 50% of the peripheral blood hematocrit defines a hemothorax, while under 50% marks a hemorrhagic effusion. His fluid hematocrit of 30% is 75% of his blood value of 40%, well over half, and trauma causes most hemothoraces. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 74, 98, 105, 108"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man has a primary spontaneous pneumothorax. His respiratory rate is 18 breaths per minute, heart rate 128 beats per minute, blood pressure 116/72 mm Hg and oxygen saturation 97% on room air, and he speaks in whole sentences. How is he classified by the American College of Chest Physicians stability definition?","choices":["Unstable: respiratory rate","Unstable: oxygen saturation","Unstable: heart rate","Stable"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The heart-rate criterion has two bounds: over 60 and under 120 beats per minute. His rate of 128 is above 120, so he fails it even though his respiratory rate of 18, normal pressure, saturation of 97% and full sentences pass; anyone who misses a criterion is unstable. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 22-year-old man with Marfan syndrome, who is tall and thin, develops sudden left-sided pleuritic chest pain and dyspnea while playing video games. He has never smoked. His upright chest radiograph shows a visceral pleural line on the left. How is his pneumothorax classified?","choices":["Primary spontaneous","Traumatic","Iatrogenic","Secondary spontaneous"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Spontaneous means no thoracic trauma, and secondary means an underlying abnormality. Connective tissue disease such as Marfan syndrome is listed among the risk factors for secondary spontaneous pneumothorax, so his tall, thin build does not make this primary. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 17, 19, 20"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman being treated with antibiotics for bacterial pneumonia still has fever and pleuritic pain after several days, and her parapneumonic effusion has grown. Thoracentesis returns thick purulent fluid. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["A longer antibiotic course","Chemical pleurodesis","Diuretic therapy","Drainage plus antibiotics"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Empyema is purulent fluid in the pleural space, and bacterial pneumonia with a parapneumonic effusion is its most common precursor. It is suspected when pneumonia does not resolve with therapy and diagnosed by purulent material on thoracentesis; treatment is drainage and antibiotics, treating the underlying disease and analgesia, and prompt therapy is critical. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 97, 107, 108"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has a new moderate left pleural effusion on chest radiograph. He has no heart, liver or kidney disease, no fever or cough, and no history of cancer or trauma, so no clinical cause is apparent. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Diuretic therapy","Diagnostic thoracentesis","Chemical pleurodesis","Indwelling pleural catheter"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Diagnostic thoracentesis is performed when there is a new pleural effusion with no clinical cause. It removes 50-100 mL for visualization and analysis, sent for protein, lactate dehydrogenase, cell count, Gram stain, cytology and culture, and ultrasound is used to guide it. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 95, 96, 97, 99, 113"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman with an inherited bleeding disorder has a first primary spontaneous pneumothorax with an apex-to-cupola distance of 2 cm. She still has significant dyspnea after adequate analgesia, a situation in which catheter aspiration is usually offered. Which feature of her history contraindicates catheter aspiration?","choices":["Her pneumothorax size","Her ongoing dyspnea","Her bleeding disorder","Her first-episode status"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A small primary spontaneous pneumothorax, under 3 cm at the apex, with significant dyspnea after analgesia is treated with catheter aspiration. Bleeding disorders are on the list of contraindications to aspiration, so her inherited bleeding disorder removes the option. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 39, 53, 54"}]};
